WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.027 --> 00:00:01.110
CARTER MORGAN: Great news.

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:02.850
Critter Junction's
game just got featured

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:05.543
as game of the century
on every app store.

00:00:05.543 --> 00:00:06.960
Let's look at how
Critter Junction

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.030
is going to handle all
of this new traffic.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:09.630
Stay tuned.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:13.032
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.795 --> 00:00:18.670
Critter Junction has
done a lot of great work

00:00:18.670 --> 00:00:21.620
to be scalable, resilient,
and highly available.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:23.780
But their traffic is set
to increase exponentially,

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.988
and they're not sure their
current infrastructure can

00:00:25.988 --> 00:00:27.160
meet those needs.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:29.410
The biggest problem is that
Critter Junction's traffic

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:32.143
has consistent spikes as people
all over the world log on when

00:00:32.143 --> 00:00:34.060
they're in the mood to
play with a virtual pet

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.770
with their friends.

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.780
To accommodate this
traffic, Critter Junction

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.240
allocated more resources
but was burning money

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.680
during non-busy hours.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:43.990
Still, that was better
than the alternative--

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:47.735
risking service outages by not
allocating enough resources.

00:00:47.735 --> 00:00:49.360
Luckily, when Critter
Junction switched

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.310
to a microservice
architecture, they

00:00:51.310 --> 00:00:53.290
decided to host their
application on Google

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.770
Kubernetes Engine,
and so they got

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:56.770
to use two Kubernetes
features that are designed

00:00:56.770 --> 00:00:58.540
to solve exactly this--

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.560
horizontal pod and
cluster autoscaling.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:05.500
In Kubernetes, you can think
of a pod as an application

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:06.123
instance.

00:01:06.123 --> 00:01:07.540
It has one or more
containers that

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:10.270
are coupled together
and treated as one unit.

00:01:10.270 --> 00:01:13.480
The horizontal pod autoscaler
automatically adds or removes

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.280
pods based on the usage
rate of your application.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:19.180
So if a lot of people are
currently using your app

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:22.250
and its computational needs
go up, it'll add pods for you.

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.460
And if that usage rate
drops, then autoscaler

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.560
will automatically remove pods.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.980
This happens based on CPU and
memory metrics by default,

00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.933
but it's also possible
to use custom metrics.

00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:34.600
This is great for
handling more requests

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.970
when you have enough
space to run more pods on.

00:01:36.970 --> 00:01:39.370
But if there's no space to
run additional pods, then

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:42.650
even if the horizontal pod
autoscaler wants to add more,

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:46.990
out of luck, unless you're
using cluster autoscaler.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.120
What this does is it
adds more resources

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.710
to your clusters-- in this case,
machines that pods can run on.

00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:55.600
We added these features
to Critter Junction's

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.600
architecture, which did help
alleviate some of the problems.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:01.780
But as we researched more, we
realized that in many cases

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:04.953
the application would run out
of memory under a heavy load.

00:02:04.953 --> 00:02:06.370
And there wasn't
a really good way

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:09.710
to estimate how much memory
was needed at any given time,

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:11.920
so we took a different approach.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:14.890
Cluster autoscaler and
horizontal pod autoscaling

00:02:14.890 --> 00:02:16.900
are both examples of
horizontal scaling,

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.330
or scaling out, which
means they focus

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:21.670
on adding more total
resources for the application

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:23.380
to use as a whole.

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.100
But sometimes it's necessary
to scale vertically, or scaling

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.710
up, by modifying the
resources provisioned

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:31.840
to an individual service,
like if a pod doesn't

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.810
have enough CPU or if
it has too much RAM.

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:38.650
That's where GKE's vertical
pod autoscaler comes in.

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:41.380
Vertical pod autoscaler
exists to automatically set

00:02:41.380 --> 00:02:43.870
up-to-date resource limits and
requests for the containers

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:44.890
in their pods.

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:48.160
It can both downscale pods that
are over-requesting resources

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:51.400
and also upscale pods that
are under-requesting resources

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.140
based on their usage over time.

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.770
This seemed like a good fit
for Critter Junction's problem,

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:58.990
so we helped them enable
the vertical pod autoscaler

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:00.610
on their cluster.

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:04.000
Then, to get a good baseline
for CPU and memory resources,

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:05.830
we used vertical
pod autoscaler's

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:08.320
sizing recommendations by
creating a vertical pod

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:11.890
autoscaler whose update
mode was set to off.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:13.810
Finally, we updated
the deployment

00:03:13.810 --> 00:03:15.580
to account for these
recommendations,

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:19.000
and Critter Junction was able
to run a lot more efficiently.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.770
Guessing what resources
your app needs

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:23.890
is tricky, but by leveraging
horizontal and vertical

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:25.330
autoscaling,
Critter Junction was

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.920
able to better manage
their workloads,

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:29.130
become more resilient,
and save money

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:30.940
on over-allocating resources.

00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:32.620
All that was left
to do was celebrate

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:35.390
their well-earned traffic boost.

00:03:35.390 --> 00:03:38.920
So that's a wrap for this
episode of "Season of Scale."

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:41.200
Stay tuned to learn how
Critter Junction continues

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:43.492
to improve the design of
their app architecture and dev

00:03:43.492 --> 00:03:44.950
processes.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.290
And, remember, always
be architecting.

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:50.640
[MUSIC PLAYING]

